 ((8
  ("Preface to the Sixth Edition" 0.0920 0.7196 0.0784 0.1273 0.1273 0.0489 0.0126 0.3178 0.1798)
  ("This new edition of Butt and Benjamin, now Butt, Benjamin and Moreira, differs in several ways" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1862 0.2027 0.2027 0.0165 0.0048 0.3860 0.0368)
  ("from earlier versions." 0.0920 0.2735 0.2027 0.2192 0.2192 0.0165 0.0048 0.3249 0.0412)
  ("• We have created some new chapters with the result that section numbers have changed" 0.1092 0.8721 0.2436 0.2601 0.2601 0.0165 0.0048 0.3331 0.0117)
  ("and the Index has been re-written." 0.1305 0.4218 0.2601 0.2766 0.2766 0.0165 0.0048 0.3079 0.0326)
  ("• We have added a large number of Mexican examples since Mexico is by far the most popu-" 0.1092 0.9024 0.2766 0.2931 0.2931 0.0165 0.0048 0.3636 0.0117)
  ("lous Spanish-speaking country and its language is of special interest to North-American" 0.1305 0.8786 0.2931 0.3095 0.3095 0.0165 0.0048 0.3177 0.0359)
  ("readers." 0.1305 0.1982 0.3096 0.3260 0.3260 0.0165 nil 0.1563 0.0677)
  ("• We have thoroughly revised the whole text, clarified it where we found it unclear, simpli-" 0.1092 0.8893 0.3261 0.3425 0.3425 0.0165 0.0048 0.3666 0.0117)
  ("fied it where it was complicated, re-written or expanded it where we had new ideas or new" 0.1305 0.9059 0.3426 0.3590 0.3590 0.0165 0.0048 0.3750 0.0326)
  ("information, and corrected it where we thought the original was misleading or inaccurate." 0.1305 0.8967 0.3591 0.3755 0.3755 0.0165 0.0048 0.3480 0.1064)
  ("• We have included new information for readers who are studying Spanish and French" 0.1092 0.8551 0.3756 0.3920 0.3920 0.0165 0.0048 0.3255 0.0117)
  ("together, this combination being especially widespread in the UK." 0.1305 0.6904 0.3921 0.4085 0.4085 0.0165 0.0048 0.3426 0.0744)
  ("• We have marked as Important points that in our experience cause problems for English-" 0.1092 0.8804 0.4086 0.4250 0.4248 0.0165 0.0048 0.3337 0.0117)
  ("speaking students, but this does not mean that the other notes should be neglected." 0.1305 0.8377 0.4250 0.4415 0.4415 0.0165 0.0048 0.3071 0.0768)
  ("• The Glossary includes Spanish translations of grammatical terms." 0.1092 0.6880 0.4415 0.4580 0.4580 0.0165 0.0048 0.3353 0.0117)
  ("Any grammar or dictionary of Spanish that aspires to be comprehensive must face the problem" 0.0920 0.9082 0.4702 0.4867 0.4867 0.0165 0.0054 0.3227 0.0369)
  ("of the international variety of the language, a problem that is much less serious in other widely-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4867 0.5032 0.5032 0.0165 0.0050 0.3341 0.0169)
  ("studied European languages like French, German and Italian." 0.0920 0.6148 0.5032 0.5197 0.5197 0.0165 0.0048 0.3102 0.0644)
  ("English has basically only two internationally recognized standards: American and ‘received’" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5319 0.5484 0.5484 0.0165 0.0071 0.2757 0.0643)
  ("British. No one would or should suggest that the varieties of other places like Australia, New" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5484 0.5649 0.5649 0.0165 0.0065 0.3015 0.0611)
  ("Zealand, South-Africa, the Caribbean or India are ‘bad’ English, but there seems to be a more" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5649 0.5814 0.5814 0.0165 0.0066 0.2964 0.0747)
  ("or less tacit agreement that foreigners should learn either American or ‘received’ British usage" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5814 0.5979 0.5979 0.0165 0.0062 0.2892 0.0181)
  ("(in practice the language of the middle and upper classes of south-east England and of those" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5979 0.6144 0.6144 0.0165 0.0069 0.2526 0.0232)
  ("who speak like them). There are however no universally recognized international standards in" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6144 0.6309 0.6309 0.0165 0.0060 0.2888 0.0378)
  ("Spanish, which differs in detail between the twenty-one countries where it is the official, or the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6309 0.6474 0.6474 0.0165 0.0056 0.3296 0.0723)
  ("main official, language. Despite the claims one hears to the contrary, none of these different varie-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6474 0.6639 0.6639 0.0165 0.0040 0.3562 0.0435)
  ("ties is accepted as a model to be followed by the others." 0.0920 0.5626 0.6639 0.6804 0.6804 0.0165 0.0048 0.3400 0.0293)
  ("It is not easy to define how much these varieties differ from one another. People who know only" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6926 0.7091 0.7091 0.0165 0.0044 0.3685 0.0129)
  ("one variety of Spanish can usually read texts and understand films and broadcasts from other" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7091 0.7256 0.7256 0.0165 0.0059 0.2813 0.0311)
  ("Spanish-speaking countries without noticing more than a few obvious peculiarities, especially" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7256 0.7421 0.7421 0.0165 0.0058 0.3016 0.1522)
  ("when the material is intended for international audiences. On the other hand, local Spanish lan-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7421 0.7586 0.7586 0.0165 0.0046 0.3393 0.0480)
  ("guage can cause problems for outsiders, and not just for people from Spain. El País Semanal of 10" 0.0920 0.9082 0.7586 0.7751 0.7750 0.0165 0.0043 0.3572 0.0523)
  ("May 2015 describes how the late Mexican poet José Emilio Pacheco met blank incomprehension" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7751 0.7916 0.7916 0.0165 0.0047 0.3558 0.0388)
  ("when he asked the receptionist in a Madrid hotel for ‘un plomero para componer la llave de la" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7916 0.8081 0.8081 0.0165 0.0054 0.3367 0.0480)
  ("tina’ (‘a plumber to fix the bath tap/faucet’); a Spaniard would have said ‘un fontanero para" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8081 0.8246 0.8246 0.0165 0.0067 0.2856 0.0385)
  ("reparar el grifo de la bañera’. Fortunately, these misunderstandings mainly affect vocabulary." 0.0920 0.9081 0.8246 0.8411 0.8411 0.0165 0.0065 0.2760 0.0631)
  ("The syntax of Pacheco’s question is perfectly ‘standard’, and as far as grammar is concerned" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8411 0.8576 0.8576 0.0165 0.0068 0.2609 0.0324)
  ("the differences between regions and countries are not striking. Spanish is still very much one" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8576 0.8741 0.8741 0.0165 0.0064 0.2775 0.0269)
  ("language." 0.0920 0.1759 0.8741 0.8906 0.8906 0.0165 nil 0.1785 0.0839)
  ("In order to make this book as useful as possible to students of all the varieties of Spanish, we" 0.0920 0.9081 0.9028 0.9193 0.9193 0.0165 0.0065 0.2790 0.0177)
  ("have selected the Latin-American content in line with the policy of previous editions of this book" 0.0920 0.9080 0.9193 0.9358 0.9358 0.0165 0.0043 0.3484 0.0409))
  (9
  ("viii Preface to the Sixth Edition" 0.0905 0.3382 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.3163 0.0310)
  ("of quoting examples which, unless otherwise stated, are also good European Spanish and there-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.0832 0.0997 0.0997 0.0165 0.0050 0.3130 0.0169)
  ("fore worthy of imitation by readers studying the language of Spain as well as of Latin America." 0.0920 0.9081 0.0997 0.1162 0.1162 0.0165 0.0053 0.3363 0.0334)
  ("We usually in fact include Latin-American examples that show that their language is the same" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1162 0.1327 0.1327 0.0165 0.0060 0.3330 0.0267)
  ("as that of Spain and that their syntax is therefore presumably acceptable everywhere or almost" 0.0920 0.9080 0.1327 0.1492 0.1492 0.0165 0.0058 0.2879 0.0178)
  ("everywhere." 0.0920 0.1974 0.1492 0.1657 0.1657 0.0165 nil 0.2332 0.1055)
  ("However, we cannot guarantee that all our examples of European Spanish are good Latin-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.1779 0.1943 0.1943 0.0165 0.0087 0.2452 0.0829)
  ("American Spanish, particularly as far as their vocabulary is concerned. Translating the Spanish" 0.0920 0.9080 0.1944 0.2108 0.2108 0.0165 0.0057 0.2816 0.0838)
  ("of Spain into ‘Latin-American Spanish’ is often impossible because there is no single ‘Latin-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2109 0.2273 0.2273 0.0165 0.0079 0.2790 0.0169)
  ("American Spanish’. To cite one well-known example, ‘pavement’/‘sidewalk’ is la acera in Spain" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2274 0.2438 0.2437 0.0165 0.0055 0.3564 0.0838)
  ("and some parts of Latin America, la vereda in the Southern Cone, el andén in Colombia, la banqueta" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2439 0.2603 0.2602 0.0165 0.0043 0.3726 0.0326)
  ("in Mexico and, according to the dictionaries, la orilla in some other American republics, and there" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2603 0.2768 0.2767 0.0165 0.0043 0.3727 0.0168)
  ("may be other regional words that we do not know." 0.0920 0.5214 0.2768 0.2933 0.2933 0.0165 0.0048 0.3527 0.0373)
  ("As far as the language of the examples is concerned, we have tried to confine ourselves to" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3055 0.3220 0.3220 0.0165 0.0083 0.2399 0.0232)
  ("plain everyday Spanish prose that can loosely be described as ‘educated informal’. However," 0.0920 0.9081 0.3220 0.3385 0.3385 0.0165 0.0071 0.2680 0.0436)
  ("we include a good deal of information about popular Spanish syntax since learners are bound" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3385 0.3550 0.3550 0.0165 0.0062 0.2716 0.0253)
  ("to encounter it in films, novels and everyday conversation and they will need to know about" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3550 0.3715 0.3715 0.0165 0.0068 0.2776 0.0168)
  ("whether to imitate it or not." 0.0920 0.3261 0.3715 0.3880 0.3880 0.0165 0.0048 0.3917 0.0708)
  ("The dividing line between syntax and lexicon is blurred in any language, and this book contains" 0.0920 0.9082 0.4002 0.4167 0.4167 0.0165 0.0048 0.3125 0.0322)
  ("a number of points that are really more appropriate for a dictionary than for a grammar book." 0.0920 0.9080 0.4167 0.4332 0.4332 0.0165 0.0060 0.3060 0.0096)
  ("But lack of space prevents us from competing with dictionaries when it comes to defining mean-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4332 0.4497 0.4497 0.0165 0.0046 0.3689 0.0297)
  ("ings, so when in doubt readers should check our translations – particularly those of individual" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4497 0.4662 0.4662 0.0165 0.0058 0.2810 0.0405)
  ("words – in a good Spanish-English dictionary." 0.0920 0.4826 0.4662 0.4827 0.4827 0.0165 0.0048 0.3113 0.0538)
  ("The difference between British and American English has also sometimes caused us some anxi-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4949 0.5114 0.5114 0.0165 0.0054 0.3300 0.0322)
  ("ety, and we hope that our British dialect will not cause too much trouble across the Atlantic. We" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5114 0.5279 0.5279 0.0165 0.0052 0.3556 0.0289)
  ("sometimes supply American equivalents of our British English where we think that the latter" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5279 0.5444 0.5444 0.0165 0.0069 0.3164 0.0912)
  ("may cause confusion, for example ‘torch’/US ‘flashlight’, ‘potato crisps’/US ‘chips’, but we have" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5444 0.5609 0.5609 0.0165 0.0043 0.3675 0.0373)
  ("not been systematic about this because we are not fluent in American English. We also hope" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5609 0.5774 0.5774 0.0165 0.0073 0.2920 0.0280)
  ("that American readers will forgive our spellings such as ‘colour’, ‘neighbour’, ‘centre’, ‘metre’," 0.0920 0.9081 0.5774 0.5939 0.5939 0.0165 0.0060 0.3269 0.0334)
  ("‘defence’, ‘traveller’, ‘cancelled’, ‘to fulfil’, ‘to practise’ (the noun in Britain is ‘practice’), ‘preter-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5939 0.6104 0.6104 0.0165 0.0051 0.3082 0.0811)
  ("ite’ and other British forms." 0.0920 0.3259 0.6104 0.6269 0.6269 0.0165 0.0048 0.3537 0.0265)
  ("Carmen Benjamin has retired from the fray after many years of hard labour on the previous" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6391 0.6556 0.6556 0.0165 0.0074 0.2797 0.0683)
  ("editions and Antonia Moreira has brought a fresh pair of eyes and ears to the project and made" 0.0920 0.9082 0.6556 0.6721 0.6721 0.0165 0.0054 0.3152 0.0684)
  ("countless valuable suggestions. We are especially grateful to Mikko Takala, whose computer" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6721 0.6886 0.6886 0.0165 0.0077 0.2860 0.0793)
  ("wizardry more than once rescued us in moments of frustration. We again offer our heartfelt" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6886 0.7051 0.7051 0.0165 0.0076 0.2896 0.0783)
  ("thanks to the many persons, English-speaking and Spanish-speaking, who have contributed to" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7051 0.7216 0.7216 0.0165 0.0059 0.2770 0.0572)
  ("this book over the years – and particularly to Carmen Benjamin – but as always the authors alone" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7216 0.7381 0.7381 0.0165 0.0042 0.3554 0.0313)
  ("are responsible for any errors or omissions." 0.0920 0.4586 0.7381 0.7546 0.7546 0.0165 0.0048 0.2978 0.0261)
  ("John Butt" 0.8279 0.9081 0.7668 0.7832 0.7832 0.0165 0.0048 0.2911 0.0393)
  ("Antonia Moreira Rodríguez" 0.6715 0.9081 0.7833 0.7997 0.7997 0.0165 0.0048 0.3414 0.0694)
  ("London UK, 2018" 0.7591 0.9081 0.7998 0.8162 0.8162 0.0165 0.0048 0.3080 0.0670))
