  ("plur. plural" 0.1093 0.2055 0.6311 0.6476 0.6476 0.0165 0.0048 0.3451 0.0398)
  ("sing. singular" 0.1093 0.2247 0.6476 0.6641 0.6641 0.0165 0.0048 0.3125 0.0405)
  ("/ indicates alternatives with the same or very similar meaning, e.g. en vano/en balde ‘in vain’," 0.1093 0.8924 0.6641 0.6806 0.6805 0.0165 0.0048 0.3579 0.0117)
  ("yo no sabía que fuera/fuese verdad ‘I didn’t know it was true’, or alternatives that are possible" 0.1093 0.8753 0.6806 0.6971 0.6970 0.0165 0.0048 0.3196 0.0182)
  ("translations, e.g. su libro = ‘her/his/your/their book’, ‘I went’ = fui/iba/he ido." 0.1093 0.7608 0.6971 0.7136 0.7135 0.0165 0.0048 0.3332 0.1047)
  ("One or more asterisks before an example show that it is badly formed and should be avoided, e.g." 0.0920 0.9082 0.7258 0.7423 0.7423 0.0165 0.0040 0.3789 0.0356)
  ("*produció (for produjo), **el mujer." 0.0920 0.3811 0.7423 0.7588 0.7587 0.0165 0.0048 0.3379 0.0849)
  ("A preceding question mark shows that the form is controversial or doubtful: ?se los dije, ?habían" 0.0920 0.9082 0.7710 0.7875 0.7874 0.0165 0.0055 0.3255 0.0150)
  ("muchos alumnos." 0.0920 0.2272 0.7876 0.8039 0.8039 0.0163 0.0048 0.3101 0.0592)
  ("Bracketed items in unattributed quotations can be deleted without a significant change of mean-" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8162 0.8327 0.8327 0.0165 0.0047 0.3299 0.0848)
  ("ing, as in debe (de) ser el cartero ‘it must be the postman’." 0.0920 0.5605 0.8327 0.8492 0.8491 0.0165 0.0048 0.3557 0.0323)
  ("‘Colloquial’ refers to language that is acceptable in relaxed educated speech but avoided in" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8614 0.8779 0.8779 0.0165 0.0082 0.2290 0.0998)
  ("formal situations. ‘Familiar’ describes language that may be heard even from educated speakers" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8779 0.8944 0.8944 0.0165 0.0047 0.3346 0.0568)
  ("in informal situations but should be used cautiously by non-fluent foreigners. ‘Popular’ describes" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8944 0.9109 0.9109 0.0165 0.0038 0.3395 0.0168)
  ("forms that some speakers may reject as ‘uneducated’ and which foreign learners should avoid." 0.0920 0.8953 0.9109 0.9274 0.9274 0.0165 0.0048 0.3302 0.0497))
  (11
  ("x Abbreviations and conventions" 0.0905 0.3491 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.2803 0.0124)
  ("‘Dialogue’ shows that the words quoted are spoken by fictional characters whose opinions and" 0.0920 0.9081 0.0832 0.0997 0.0997 0.0165 0.0056 0.2994 0.0885)
  ("language, which are sometimes comical, sexist or in some other way outrageous, should not be" 0.0920 0.9081 0.0997 0.1162 0.1162 0.0165 0.0055 0.3408 0.0831)
  ("attributed to their author." 0.0920 0.3083 0.1162 0.1327 0.1327 0.0165 0.0048 0.2969 0.0849)
  ("We use the term ‘Latin America(n)’ rather than ‘Spanish America(n)’ since it should be obvious" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1449 0.1613 0.1613 0.0165 0.0055 0.3490 0.0267)
  ("that we are not referring to Brazil or to the French-speaking territories, and because the term" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1614 0.1778 0.1778 0.0165 0.0069 0.2411 0.0334)
  ("‘Spanish American’ potentially annoys Latin Americans as much as ‘British American’ would no" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1779 0.1943 0.1943 0.0165 0.0045 0.3789 0.0729)
  ("doubt irritate Americans and Canadians." 0.0920 0.4391 0.1944 0.2108 0.2108 0.0165 0.0048 0.3275 0.0508)
  ("The spelling of Spanish words reflects the Spanish Academy’s latest recommendations, espe-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2230 0.2395 0.2395 0.0165 0.0073 0.2908 0.0322)
  ("cially noticeable in such words as guion for guión, rio for rió, crie for crié, etc. (see 44.2.4). In the case" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2395 0.2560 0.2559 0.0165 0.0039 0.3503 0.0457)
  ("of unresolved disputes, e.g. whether one should write an accent on the pronouns este/éste, ese/ése" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2560 0.2725 0.2724 0.0165 0.0048 0.3164 0.0169)
  ("and aquel/aquél and on the adverb solo/sólo, we show both forms but recommend the Academy’s" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2725 0.2890 0.2889 0.0165 0.0052 0.3478 0.0326)
  ("advice, which is to omit the accent." 0.0920 0.3889 0.2890 0.3055 0.3055 0.0165 0.0048 0.3708 0.0605)
  ("On hearing, out of context, a verb form like habla ‘she/he/you/it speak(s)’, Spanish-speakers do" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3177 0.3342 0.3341 0.0165 0.0042 0.3311 0.0264)
  ("not automatically form a mental image of a male grammatical subject. For this reason we trans-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3342 0.3507 0.3507 0.0165 0.0049 0.3863 0.0282)
  ("late such forms by ‘(s)he speaks’ even though the other possibilities − ‘you speak’ (usted) and ‘it" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3507 0.3672 0.3672 0.0165 0.0047 0.3178 0.0310)
  ("speaks’ − are not usually shown. If only ‘she’ or ‘he’ appears in the translation of an attributed" 0.0920 0.9082 0.3672 0.3837 0.3837 0.0165 0.0054 0.3012 0.0634)
  ("example this reflects the gender of the character in the original text. When a third-person plural" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3837 0.4002 0.4002 0.0165 0.0048 0.3314 0.0728)
  ("verb appears without a pronoun, e.g. reciben ‘they receive’, it must be remembered that the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4002 0.4167 0.4166 0.0165 0.0077 0.2907 0.0387)
  ("translation could also be ‘you receive’ (ustedes reciben) if the meaning of the sentence or phrase" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4167 0.4332 0.4331 0.0165 0.0055 0.2808 0.0927)
  ("allows it." 0.0920 0.1699 0.4332 0.4497 0.4497 0.0165 0.0050 0.4177 0.0561)
  ("Phonetic Symbols" 0.0920 0.2666 0.4765 0.4938 0.4938 0.0173 0.0056 0.3168 0.0845)
  ("Spanish pronunciation is roughly indicated as follows. Previous editions adopted the International" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5022 0.5187 0.5187 0.0165 0.0027 0.3579 0.0675)
  ("Phonetic Alphabet (IPA), but some IPA signs were confusing for students:" 0.0920 0.7172 0.5187 0.5352 0.5352 0.0165 0.0048 0.3479 0.0742)
  ("Symbol" 0.1092 0.1777 0.5474 0.5636 0.5636 0.0162 nil 0.1934 0.0685)
  ("Phonetic description" 0.2156 0.3970 0.5474 0.5636 0.5636 0.0162 0.0048 0.2554 0.0770)
  ("Remarks" 0.4971 0.5752 0.5474 0.5636 0.5636 0.0162 nil 0.2739 0.0781)
  ("β" 0.1092 0.1190 0.5628 0.5807 0.5807 0.0179 nil nil 0.0098)
  ("voiced bilabial fricative" 0.2156 0.4146 0.5639 0.5804 0.5804 0.0165 0.0048 0.2912 0.0565)
  ("Air released steadily through lips held as for" 0.4971 0.8760 0.5639 0.5804 0.5804 0.0165 0.0048 0.3176 0.0282)
  ("		  English b" 0.3710 0.5961 0.5804 0.5969 0.5969 0.0165 0.0144 1.3288 0.3927)
  ("χ" 0.1092 0.1177 0.5958 0.6137 0.6137 0.0179 nil nil 0.0086)
  ("voiceless velar fricative" 0.2156 0.4140 0.5969 0.6134 0.6134 0.0165 0.0048 0.2540 0.0759)
  ("Like ch of German lachen or of Scottish ‘loch’" 0.4971 0.8747 0.5969 0.6134 0.6133 0.0165 0.0048 0.3393 0.0373)
  ("γ" 0.1092 0.1177 0.6123 0.6302 0.6302 0.0179 nil nil 0.0085)
  ("voiced velar fricative" 0.2156 0.3946 0.6134 0.6299 0.6299 0.0165 0.0048 0.2744 0.0565)
  ("Air released steadily through the throat held as" 0.4971 0.8984 0.6134 0.6299 0.6299 0.0165 0.0048 0.3085 0.0282)
  ("		  for English g in ‘ago’" 0.3710 0.6925 0.6299 0.6464 0.6463 0.0165 0.0048 1.7260 0.3927)
  ("θ" 0.1092 0.1184 0.6453 0.6632 0.6632 0.0179 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("voiceless interdental fricative" 0.2156 0.4643 0.6464 0.6629 0.6629 0.0165 0.0048 0.2673 0.0759)
  ("Like th of ‘think’" 0.4971 0.6376 0.6464 0.6629 0.6628 0.0165 0.0048 0.3299 0.0373)
  ("ð" 0.1092 0.1197 0.6629 0.6794 0.6794 0.0165 nil nil 0.0105)
  ("voiced interdental fricative" 0.2156 0.4449 0.6629 0.6794 0.6794 0.0165 0.0048 0.2822 0.0565)
  ("Like th of ‘this’" 0.4971 0.6238 0.6629 0.6794 0.6793 0.0165 0.0048 0.3238 0.0373)
  ("ʎ" 0.1092 0.1188 0.6783 0.6962 0.6962 0.0179 nil nil 0.0096)
  ("voiced palatal lateral	Palatalized l, as in Spanish llamo. Tongue flat" 0.2156 0.8759 0.6794 0.6958 0.6958 0.0165 0.0048 0.8283 0.0565)
  ("against roof of mouth" 0.5164 0.7003 0.6959 0.7123 0.7123 0.0165 0.0048 0.3493 0.0612)
  ("ñ" 0.1092 0.1204 0.7124 0.7288 0.7288 0.0165 nil nil 0.0112)
  ("voiced palatal nasal" 0.2156 0.3840 0.7124 0.7288 0.7288 0.0165 0.0048 0.2697 0.0565)
  ("Like gn in French cognac. Tongue flat against" 0.4971 0.8736 0.7124 0.7288 0.7287 0.0165 0.0048 0.2865 0.0373)
  ("		  roof of mouth (IPA ɲ)" 0.3710 0.6967 0.7278 0.7457 0.7453 0.0165 0.0048 1.6716 0.3927)
  ("ŋ" 0.1092 0.1187 0.7443 0.7622 0.7622 0.0179 nil nil 0.0095)
  ("voiced velar nasal" 0.2156 0.3690 0.7454 0.7618 0.7618 0.0165 0.0048 0.2535 0.0565)
  ("Like ng in American and Southern British ‘sing’" 0.4971 0.9014 0.7454 0.7618 0.7617 0.0165 0.0048 0.3427 0.0373)
  ("		  (not as in ‘finger’)" 0.3710 0.6688 0.7619 0.7783 0.7783 0.0165 0.0048 1.7242 0.3927)
  ("ɾ" 0.1092 0.1156 0.7773 0.7952 0.7952 0.0179 nil nil 0.0064)
  ("voiced alveolar tap or flap	r pronounced with a single flap of the tongue as" 0.2156 0.9046 0.7784 0.7948 0.7947 0.0165 0.0048 0.4756 0.0565)
  ("in Spanish caro" 0.5164 0.6424 0.7948 0.8113 0.8112 0.0165 0.0048 0.2875 0.0168)
  ("rr" 0.1092 0.1244 0.8113 0.8278 0.8278 0.0165 nil nil 0.0152)
  ("voiced alveolar trill" 0.2156 0.3812 0.8113 0.8278 0.8278 0.0165 0.0048 0.2855 0.0565)
  ("Rolled r as in Spanish carro (IPA r)" 0.4971 0.7863 0.8113 0.8278 0.8277 0.0165 0.0048 0.3373 0.0556)
  ("y" 0.1092 0.1199 0.8278 0.8443 0.8443 0.0165 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("voiced palatal approximant" 0.2156 0.4500 0.8278 0.8443 0.8443 0.0165 0.0048 0.3092 0.0565)
  ("Like y in ‘yes’ (IPA j)" 0.4971 0.6714 0.8278 0.8443 0.8442 0.0165 0.0048 0.3904 0.0373)
  ("ch" 0.1092 0.1290 0.8443 0.8608 0.8608 0.0165 nil nil 0.0198)
  ("voiceless palatal stop" 0.2156 0.3957 0.8443 0.8608 0.8608 0.0165 0.0048 0.2641 0.0759)
  ("Like ch in ‘mischief’ (IPA tʃ)" 0.4971 0.7321 0.8443 0.8608 0.8607 0.0165 0.0048 0.3992 0.0373)
  ("A dash separates syllables and the stressed syllable is marked with an accent: [a-βlá-mos] =" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8852 0.9017 0.9017 0.0165 0.0079 0.2709 0.0150)
  ("hablamos; see 44.5." 0.0920 0.2436 0.9017 0.9182 0.9181 0.0165 0.0048 0.3053 0.0769))
  (12
  ("Abbreviations and conventions xi" 0.6445 0.9088 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.2801 0.1077)
  ("[aw] is like the ‘ow’ in English ‘cow’; [ay] is like the ‘i’ in English ‘high’; [ey] is like the ‘ay’ in" 0.0920 0.9081 0.0832 0.0997 0.0997 0.0165 0.0061 0.3392 0.0385)
  ("‘hay’; [oy] is like the ‘oy’ of ‘boy’; [ew] is like the ‘e’ of ‘egg’ followed by ‘w’; [w] is the English" 0.0920 0.9082 0.0997 0.1162 0.1162 0.0165 0.0056 0.3606 0.0471)
  ("‘w’ but with well-rounded lips. Other signs should be given their usual Spanish pronunciation." 0.0920 0.9005 0.1162 0.1327 0.1327 0.0165 0.0048 0.3445 0.0268)
  ("[ñ] must be distinguished in pronunciation from [ny] as in words like uranio [u-ɾá-nyo] ‘uranium’" 0.0920 0.9080 0.1449 0.1613 0.1613 0.0165 0.0041 0.3744 0.0240)
  ("and huraño [u-ɾá-ño] ‘grumpy’/‘unsociable’." 0.0920 0.4674 0.1614 0.1778 0.1777 0.0165 0.0048 0.3069 0.0326)
  ("[ʎ] must be distinguished from [ly] in words like pollo [pó-ʎo] ‘chicken’ (for cooking) and polio" 0.0920 0.9082 0.1901 0.2065 0.2064 0.0165 0.0061 0.3155 0.0225)
  ("[pó-lyo] ‘polio’ (the disease)." 0.0920 0.3352 0.2065 0.2230 0.2230 0.0165 0.0048 0.3019 0.0682))
  (13
  ("" nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil))
  (14
  ("1 Gender of nouns" 0.0920 0.5341 0.0777 0.1275 0.1275 0.0489 0.0126 0.2858 0.0252)
  ("The main points discussed in this chapter are" 0.0920 0.4760 0.1846 0.2011 0.2011 0.0165 0.0048 0.3270 0.0322)
  ("• Gender of nouns referring to humans and some animals (Section 1.2)" 0.1092 0.7153 0.2113 0.2278 0.2278 0.0165 0.0048 0.3368 0.0117)
  ("• Gender of nouns referring to lifeless things, plants and other animals (Section 1.3)" 0.1092 0.8245 0.2278 0.2443 0.2443 0.0165 0.0048 0.3182 0.0117)
  ("• The gender of foreign words (Section 1.3.12)" 0.1092 0.5055 0.2443 0.2608 0.2608 0.0165 0.0048 0.3043 0.0117)
  ("• Doubtful genders (Section 1.3.15)" 0.1092 0.4129 0.2608 0.2773 0.2773 0.0165 0.0048 0.2949 0.0117)
  ("• Misleading genders of some French nouns (Section 1.4)" 0.1092 0.5990 0.2773 0.2938 0.2938 0.0165 0.0048 0.3220 0.0117)
  ("1.1 Gender of nouns: general" 0.0920 0.4879 0.3221 0.3463 0.3463 0.0243 0.0079 0.2709 0.0395)
  ("Spanish nouns are either masculine or feminine except for a few nouns of undecided gender" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3533 0.3698 0.3698 0.0165 0.0071 0.2616 0.0675)
  ("listed at 1.3.15. The whole question of the gender of Spanish nouns becomes clearer if we divide" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3698 0.3863 0.3863 0.0165 0.0050 0.3313 0.0467)
  ("them into two groups:" 0.0920 0.2813 0.3863 0.4028 0.4028 0.0165 0.0048 0.3725 0.0437)
  ("(A) Nouns that refer to human beings and to a few well-known animals: Section 1.2." 0.0920 0.8063 0.4150 0.4315 0.4315 0.0165 0.0048 0.3763 0.0278)
  ("(B) Nouns that refer to lifeless things, to plants and to the animals not included in group A:" 0.0920 0.9079 0.4437 0.4601 0.4601 0.0165 0.0073 0.2839 0.0246)
  ("Section 1.3." 0.0920 0.1872 0.4602 0.4766 0.4766 0.0165 0.0048 0.2763 0.0615)
  ("1.2 Group A: gender of nouns referring to human beings" 0.0920 0.8569 0.5049 0.5292 0.5292 0.0243 0.0079 0.3158 0.0395)
  ("and to a few animals" 0.1456 0.4279 0.5279 0.5521 0.5521 0.0243 0.0079 0.3994 0.0505)
  ("As one might expect, nouns that denote males are masculine, and nouns referring to females" 0.0920 0.9082 0.5591 0.5756 0.5756 0.0165 0.0070 0.2957 0.0232)
  ("are feminine, so el hombre ‘man’, la mujer ‘woman’, el toro ‘bull’, la vaca ‘cow’. This rule applies to" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5756 0.5921 0.5920 0.0165 0.0049 0.4011 0.0261)
  ("almost all human beings but only to a few animals, many of them listed in 1.2.1. The gender of" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5921 0.6086 0.6086 0.0165 0.0056 0.3635 0.0572)
  ("other animals is discussed at 1.3.1." 0.0920 0.3838 0.6086 0.6251 0.6251 0.0165 0.0048 0.3372 0.0448)
  ("The gender of the nouns in group A is more logical in Spanish than in French, where the mas-" 0.0920 0.9079 0.6373 0.6538 0.6538 0.0165 0.0060 0.3088 0.0322)
  ("culine noun le professeur can refer to a woman. Forms like la recluta ‘recruit’, la centinela ‘sentry’" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6538 0.6703 0.6702 0.0165 0.0057 0.3165 0.0518)
  ("were applied to men in the past, but we now say el recluta, el centinela for a man and la recluta, la" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6703 0.6868 0.6867 0.0165 0.0051 0.3711 0.0418)
  ("centinela for a woman." 0.0920 0.2797 0.6868 0.7033 0.7032 0.0165 0.0048 0.3790 0.0699)
  ("Exceptions: a few nouns of fixed gender like la víctima or la celebridad may refer to males or to" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7155 0.7320 0.7317 0.0165 0.0062 0.3073 0.1000)
  ("females: see 1.2.11 for a list." 0.0920 0.3245 0.7320 0.7484 0.7484 0.0165 0.0048 0.3474 0.0701)
  ("(1) Note that usually the plural masculine form of these nouns is used for mixed sex groups: los" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7607 0.7771 0.7769 0.0165 0.0052 0.3403 0.0225)
  ("gatos = ‘cats’ as well as ‘tom cats’, mis tíos = ‘my aunt(s) and uncle(s)’ as well as ‘my uncles’, los" 0.0920 0.9082 0.7772 0.7936 0.7935 0.0165 0.0055 0.3688 0.0406)
  ("padres = ‘parents’ as well as ‘fathers’. See 1.2.8." 0.0920 0.4842 0.7937 0.8101 0.8100 0.0165 0.0048 0.3195 0.0499)
  ("1.2.1 Special forms for male and female" 0.0920 0.5543 0.8342 0.8550 0.8550 0.0208 0.0068 0.3889 0.0541)
  ("As in English, some nouns have special forms for the male and for the female and they must be" 0.0920 0.9079 0.8627 0.8792 0.8792 0.0165 0.0052 0.3567 0.0232)
  ("learned separately. The following list is not exhaustive:" 0.0920 0.5588 0.8792 0.8957 0.8957 0.0165 0.0048 0.3137 0.0643)
  ("el abad/la abadesa abbot/abbess" 0.1092 0.3728 0.9058 0.9223 0.9222 0.0165 0.0048 0.2322 0.0128)
  ("el actor/la actriz actor/actress" 0.1092 0.3567 0.9223 0.9388 0.9387 0.0165 0.0048 0.2370 0.0128)
  ("el barón/la baronesa baron/baroness" 0.5233 0.8176 0.9058 0.9223 0.9222 0.0165 0.0048 0.2346 0.0128)
  ("el caballo/la yegua stallion/mare" 0.5233 0.7868 0.9223 0.9388 0.9387 0.0165 0.0048 0.3274 0.0128))))
